The main reason is legal requirements and store rules that Zen, by design, does not meet:
• moderation: stores require the creator to review reports and remove content and accounts personally, and Zen has no server and nobody in the middle who could do that;
• currency and the pyramid wallet: financial services and cryptocurrencies require a company and financial licences in the stores;
• paid gates: fees for digital services in an app must go through the store’s payments, with its 15–30% commission;
• dating: requires age verification and a moderation team;
• self-updates and background work: Google Play forbids an app from updating itself from outside the store, and the App Store heavily restricts background activity;
• creator identity: stores publish the creator’s details and require formalities (including an encryption declaration).

Then there are the costs: the App Store is 99 USD a year, Google Play 25 USD once, and licences and legal support run into the thousands. Zen is made by one person, for free. A store could also remove the app at any moment or demand changes, and Zen is meant to be independent. Besides, nobody, not even a store, knows you have Zen.

That’s why you get Zen from the project’s official page on GitHub or from a friend (“Pass it on”).

